{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST be the home of deriving an initial requirement set from existing code.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.cli.skill_structure.bootstrap` has two parts. The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill must contain the direction for deriving an initial requirement set from existing code. Under the parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure`, that skill must be the only home for the topic, so no other skill may state it in full.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_bootstrap_skill_is_home_of_deriving_an_initial_requirement_set]` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It covers both parts.\n- **Content.** The test reads the skill's `SKILL.md` with `skill_md`. For each of `## Inventories`, `## Proposing requirements`, `## Tagging` and `## Report`, it calls `section`, which asserts the heading exists. The four headings cover the derivation workflow. The inventories come first, then proposing, tagging and reporting.\n- **Home.** `assert_home(\"habeas-bootstrap-requirements\")` checks the markers from `home_markers`: \"every first-party runnable test or test case\", \"## Proposing requirements\" and \"Independent recount:\". It uses `assert_phrases` over the whole skill (`SKILL.md` plus `references/`) to assert the home states each one. It then loops over every other skill in `SKILLS` and asserts none contains any of those markers, with the message \"{other} restates {home}'s {marker}\". This enforces the \"exactly one skill, its home\" condition from the parent for this topic.\n\n**Failures it would catch.** The test fails in three cases:\n- The bootstrap skill drops one of the workflow sections.\n- The bootstrap skill drops the marker phrases.\n- Another skill, such as `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence` or `habeas-refactor`, copies a bootstrap marker.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. The requirement is broad and says nothing more specific about content. The headings and markers are a reasonable proxy for it. Cross-reference behaviour belongs to the sibling requirement `skill_structure.refer`, which has its own test. There are no acknowledgments, and none are needed.",
